// Rounding, totals, and balancing of a `Period` from a starting point, against reference data.
//
// The rows come from a generator that computes each answer by the rules of
// `Temporal.Duration.prototype.round` and `total` of the current ECMAScript Temporal
// specification (RoundRelativeDuration with its Nudge and Bubble operations), over the time zone
// data of V8. V8's own Temporal in Node 22 has the rules of an older draft for these functions,
// so its answers are not the reference.
//
// The rows differ from the specification in three places:
// - The period between two zoned values has the two additions that
//   `corpus/native/stdlib-time-rounding` describes.
// - A starting point in a zone stands for itself, also when it is the later of two equal local
//   times.
// - Where the specification asserts that the point reached is between the two values of the
//   rounded unit, and it is not (February 29 plus 3 years, and a period that ends a little after
//   that February 28), the point is held between them.
//
// Each row is tab-separated. After its kind it has the starting point in four fields: `d`, `-`,
// a date, and `-`; or `z`, a zone, and an instant as seconds and nanoseconds. A period follows,
// and the last field is the answer, or `!` when there is none.
// - `r`: the largest unit (`y`, `m`, `w`, `d`, `t` for only a time part, `a` when it is not
//   given), the kind of the smallest unit (`u` for a unit of the calendar, `s` for a step of
//   time), the unit, the count, and the mode: the rounded period.
// - `t`: a unit: the total in that unit times one million, rounded down.
// - `b`: the largest unit: the period in those units.
// - `s`: the exact span as seconds and nanoseconds.

